      "reasoning": "All 16 final answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. q-317898: the graph is negative, increasing, approaches y=0 from below, and passes through (-1,-1); option 1 matches. q-317895: the graph is negative, increasing, approaches y=0 from below, and passes through (1,-1); option 4 matches. These choices are the unlabeled versions of the respective final worked-solution graphs. Both graph solutions contain an explanatory error: a horizontally translated point is incorrectly called the y-intercept. Before reflection, the actual new y-intercepts are (0,3/10) and (0,3/2), respectively; their final graphs and answers remain correct. q-165971: substituting x=0 gives 4+1=5. q-92930: substituting x=0 gives -5+3=-2. q-92995: e^x=e^4 implies x=4. q-92998: e^(2x-1)=2 implies x=(ln(2)+1)/2. q-92988: e^(2x) tends to zero as x tends to negative infinity, giving asymptote y=-5. q-92963: (1/3)^(2x-3) tends to zero as x tends to infinity, so a=2. q-67625: continuity gives (1-c)/3=2, hence c=-5. q-54489: an exponential with base 1/4 tends to zero as x tends to infinity. q-47073: 3x-8 tends to negative infinity, so 2^(3x-8) tends to zero. q-102107: subtracting 6 and dividing by -5 reverses the inequalities, giving 1<x<3; option 0 has the correct open endpoints. q-44761: adding 6 and dividing by -5 gives -2<x<-1; option 2 matches. q-36720: the one-sided limits are 6(1/2)+1=4 and -4 log_2(1/2)=4, so the limit is 4. q-36724: the left limit is cube root of 8=2, while the right limit is cos(3 pi)=-1, so the two-sided limit does not exist. q-37488: the left branch is tan(x), whose limit at zero is 0. No submitted grade or earlier answer was supplied; confirmation rests on the captured worked solutions and independent mathematics.",
